# Signing credentials. Copy to .env and fill in — .env is gitignored and must
# never be committed. These are only used for local signed builds; GitHub
# Actions reads the same values from repository secrets instead.
#
# Nothing here is needed for an ordinary build. Without it the app is ad-hoc
# signed, which is valid but not trusted: macOS shows "unidentified developer"
# and the user opens it with right-click → Open.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- macOS ----
# Requires membership of the Apple Developer Program and a "Developer ID
# Application" certificate. Check what you have with:
#   security find-identity -v -p codesigning
#
# The full identity string, e.g. "Developer ID Application: Name (TEAMID)".
APPLE_SIGNING_IDENTITY=

# Notarisation. Without it macOS still warns on first launch, even when signed.
# Preferred: an App Store Connect API key (.p8).
APPLE_API_ISSUER=
APPLE_API_KEY=
APPLE_API_KEY_PATH=

# Alternative to the API key: an app-specific password from appleid.apple.com.
# APPLE_ID=
# APPLE_PASSWORD=
# APPLE_TEAM_ID=

# -------------------------------------------------------------- Windows ----
# A code-signing certificate as base64 of the .pfx, which stops SmartScreen
# warning. Only used by CI; a local Windows build can use the cert directly.
# WINDOWS_CERTIFICATE=
# WINDOWS_CERTIFICATE_PASSWORD=
